      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely right that the current system is deeply flawed and often functions as a tool for the powerful to ignore their obligations while holding weaker nations accountable. The lack of real consequences for superpowers like the US or China when they violate treaties proves that international law is indeed a \"soft\" constraint that only works when it aligns with the interests of the strongest players. However, arguing that it is *just* a suggestion ignores the fact that even the most powerful nations often face significant diplomatic, economic, and reputational costs for blatant violations, which acts as a powerful deterrent. To phrase this in your brief, you should acknowledge the hypocrisy and power imbalances while arguing that the system is the only existing mechanism to check absolute power, even if it is far from perfect.",
